/* Level 18: Advanced reading (about Grade 6 to 7). Complex sentences, history, science, opinions and harder choices. */
S(18, "The Silk Road", `More than two thousand years ago, long before aeroplanes, railways or even proper roads, a vast network of trade routes stretched across Asia, linking China in the east with the Mediterranean Sea in the west. Today we call it the Silk Road, although that name was invented much later, in 1877, by a German geographer. The people who travelled it simply called it the road.

The name comes from silk, the shimmering fabric that China produced and that wealthy people in Rome and Persia were desperate to buy. For centuries, the Chinese guarded the secret of how silk was made, from the cocoons of silkworms fed on mulberry leaves. Anyone caught smuggling silkworm eggs out of the country could be executed.

Yet silk was only one of many goods. Caravans of camels carried spices, tea, paper, porcelain, jade, horses, glassware, gold and precious stones. Very few merchants travelled the entire route, which could be more than six thousand kilometres long. Instead, goods passed from trader to trader, city to city, each adding a little to the price. A roll of silk might change hands a dozen times before it reached a Roman market.

Some of the most important routes passed through the land that is now Pakistan. The ancient city of Taxila, near present-day Islamabad, was a famous centre of trade and learning. Merchants, monks and scholars from many lands met there. Further north, travellers struggled through the high passes of the Karakoram and the Hindu Kush, where snow, landslides and bandits made every journey dangerous.

Perhaps the most valuable things carried along the Silk Road could not be loaded onto a camel. Ideas travelled too. Buddhism spread from South Asia into China along these routes, and later Islam spread eastward into Central Asia. The knowledge of papermaking moved from China to Samarkand and then to Baghdad, transforming how books were produced across the Muslim world and, eventually, Europe. Mathematics, medicine, music, recipes and languages were exchanged in busy markets and roadside inns called caravanserais.

Unfortunately, not everything that travelled was welcome. In the fourteenth century, historians believe the Black Death, a terrible plague, spread westward along trade routes, killing millions of people.

The Silk Road declined after about 1450, as European sailors discovered sea routes to Asia that were faster and cheaper than crossing deserts and mountains. However, its influence never disappeared. Many modern foods, inventions and beliefs reached us because of those ancient merchants.

Today, the idea of the Silk Road has returned. New highways, railways and pipelines are being built across Asia, and some people call them a new Silk Road. Whether these projects will bring the same exchange of ideas as the old routes did remains to be seen. But the lesson of history is clear: when people from different places meet and trade, they share far more than goods.`, "geographer, cocoons, executed, caravans, porcelain, scholars, caravanserais, influence",
"Why was the trade network called the Silk Road?|Silk from China was one of its most famous and valuable goods;Why did the Silk Road decline after about 1450?|European sailors found sea routes to Asia that were faster and cheaper;What does the writer mean that ideas travelled too?|Religions, knowledge like papermaking, and other ideas spread along with the goods");

S(18, "How Vaccines Train the Body", `Every day, your body is under attack. Bacteria, viruses and other microscopic invaders, collectively called pathogens, enter through your nose, mouth and any small cut in your skin. Most of the time, you never notice, because your immune system defeats them before they can make you ill.

The immune system is an extraordinary defence force made up of different kinds of white blood cells. Some act like soldiers, swallowing and destroying invaders. Others produce antibodies, special proteins shaped to lock onto one particular pathogen and mark it for destruction. Crucially, after an infection has been defeated, some of these cells remain in the body as memory cells. If the same pathogen ever returns, the memory cells recognise it immediately and launch a much faster and stronger attack. This is why, after recovering from chickenpox, most people never catch it again.

The problem is that the first encounter can be dangerous. Some diseases, such as measles, polio or tetanus, can cause serious harm or death before the immune system learns to fight them.

A vaccine solves this problem by giving the immune system a safe rehearsal. It contains a weakened or killed form of a pathogen, a harmless piece of it, or instructions that teach the body to make that piece. The immune system responds as though it were facing a real infection and produces memory cells, but without the person becoming seriously ill. Later, if the real pathogen appears, the body is already prepared.

The idea was first tested scientifically in 1796 by an English doctor, Edward Jenner. He noticed that milkmaids who caught cowpox, a mild disease from cows, seemed to be protected from smallpox, a deadly disease that killed millions. Jenner deliberately infected a boy with cowpox and later exposed him to smallpox. The boy did not become ill. The word vaccine comes from vacca, the Latin word for cow.

Over the following two centuries, vaccines transformed human health. After a worldwide vaccination campaign, smallpox was declared completely eradicated in 1980, the first human disease ever wiped out. Polio, which once paralysed hundreds of thousands of children every year, has disappeared from almost every country.

Pakistan and Afghanistan are among the last places where wild polio still circulates. Thousands of health workers, many of them women, travel from house to house to give drops to children, sometimes in difficult and dangerous conditions. Their goal is to reach every single child, because as long as the virus survives anywhere, it can spread again.

Vaccines also protect people who cannot be vaccinated themselves, such as newborn babies or people with certain illnesses. When enough people in a community are immune, a disease cannot spread easily from person to person. This is called herd immunity.

Like all medicines, vaccines are carefully tested for safety before they are approved, and they continue to be monitored afterwards. Doctors around the world agree that they are one of the most effective tools humanity has ever developed to prevent disease.`, "pathogens, immune, antibodies, rehearsal, deliberately, eradicated, paralysed, herd immunity",
"What do memory cells do?|They recognise a pathogen that returns and launch a faster, stronger attack;Why did Jenner's experiment work?|Cowpox was mild but trained the body to fight smallpox, a similar disease;What does herd immunity mean?|When enough people are immune, a disease can't spread easily, which protects those who can't be vaccinated");

S(18, "Abdus Salam", `In 1926, in the small town of Jhang in Punjab, a boy was born who would one day help explain how the universe works. His name was Abdus Salam, and he would become the first Pakistani to win a Nobel Prize in science.

Salam's family was not wealthy. His father worked in the education department, and the town had no electricity. Salam studied by the light of an oil lamp. Even so, his brilliance was obvious from an early age. At fourteen, he achieved the highest marks ever recorded in the matriculation examination of the University of the Punjab. When he cycled home after the results were announced, the whole town came out to greet him.

He went on to study mathematics at Government College in Lahore and then won a scholarship to St John's College, Cambridge, in England. There, he earned first-class degrees in both mathematics and physics. As a research student, he solved a difficult problem in theoretical physics so quickly that his professors were astonished.

In the early 1950s, Salam returned to Pakistan, hoping to build a strong scientific tradition in his own country. But he found that there were almost no laboratories, few journals, and no other physicists to talk to. He felt intellectually isolated. Reluctantly, he returned to Britain, where he became a professor at Imperial College London.

This experience shaped the rest of his life. Salam believed that talented scientists from poorer countries should not have to choose between their homelands and their careers. In 1964, he founded the International Centre for Theoretical Physics in Trieste, Italy, where scientists from developing countries could spend time working with leading researchers before returning home. Since then, many thousands of scientists from Asia, Africa and Latin America have visited the centre.

Salam's most famous work concerned the forces of nature. Physicists had identified four fundamental forces: gravity, electromagnetism, and two forces that act inside the nucleus of atoms, called the strong and weak nuclear forces. Salam, working separately from the American physicists Sheldon Glashow and Steven Weinberg, developed a theory showing that electromagnetism and the weak force were actually two aspects of a single force, which became known as the electroweak force.

At first, the theory was only mathematics. But experiments in the 1970s and 1980s confirmed its predictions. In 1979, Salam, Glashow and Weinberg shared the Nobel Prize in Physics. Salam attended the ceremony in Stockholm wearing a traditional turban and sherwani.

Throughout his life, Salam argued that science belonged to all of humanity, not just to wealthy nations. He spoke often about the golden age of Islamic science, when scholars in Baghdad, Cairo and Cordoba led the world, and urged Muslim countries to invest in research again.

Abdus Salam died in 1996. His life raises a question that many countries still face today: how can a nation keep its most talented people, and give them the tools they need to do great work at home?`, "matriculation, theoretical, intellectually, isolated, reluctantly, fundamental, electroweak, humanity",
"Why did Salam found the centre in Trieste?|So scientists from poorer countries could work with top researchers without giving up their homelands;What did Salam's theory show?|That electromagnetism and the weak force are two parts of one force, the electroweak force;What does intellectually isolated mean?|Having no one nearby to share and discuss ideas with");

S(18, "Should Homework Be Banned?", `Few topics cause as much argument among students, parents and teachers as homework. Some schools in different parts of the world have reduced or even abolished it, especially for younger children. Others believe it is essential. Who is right? Let us look at both sides of the argument before reaching a conclusion.

Those who support homework make several strong points. First, practice is necessary for learning. Just as a cricketer cannot become skilful by attending one training session a week, a student cannot master multiplication or spelling only by listening in class. Homework provides time to practise skills until they become automatic.

Second, homework teaches responsibility and independence. A student who must organise their time, remember deadlines and work without a teacher's help is developing habits that will be valuable at university and in employment.

Third, homework can involve families. When parents see what their children are studying, they can support them and notice early if they are struggling.

However, critics of homework raise serious concerns. Research suggests that for young children, the benefits of homework are small, while the costs can be significant. Long hours of homework may cause stress, reduce sleep, and take away time for play, exercise, reading for pleasure and family life, all of which are also important for healthy development.

Critics also point out that homework can increase inequality. A child whose parents are educated, who has a quiet room, books and a reliable internet connection, has a large advantage over a child who must help at home, share a crowded room with siblings, or whose parents cannot read. In such cases, homework may measure a family's circumstances more than a student's effort.

Finally, some argue that badly designed homework, such as copying out pages or repeating tasks already mastered, wastes time and makes students dislike learning.

So what is the answer? Perhaps the question itself is too simple. The evidence suggests that the amount and type of homework matter far more than whether it exists at all. A small amount of meaningful homework, such as reading a book, practising a new skill, or investigating a question, seems to be beneficial, particularly for older students. Huge piles of repetitive worksheets do not.

Many educators now recommend a guideline of roughly ten minutes per night for each school year. Under this rule, a student in Year 1 might do ten minutes, while a student in Year 7 might do around seventy. Others suggest that homework for young children should consist mainly of reading.

In my opinion, homework should not be banned, but it should be reformed. Teachers should set tasks that are short, purposeful and achievable without expensive resources or adult help. Schools could offer quiet homework clubs for students who have nowhere to work at home. And students, for their part, should remember that the goal is not to finish a task but to learn something.

What do you think? The best way to decide is to examine the evidence, consider who is affected, and form your own opinion.`, "abolished, essential, automatic, inequality, circumstances, repetitive, reformed, purposeful",
"What are two reasons some people support homework?|It gives practice, teaches responsibility, and involves families;How can homework increase inequality?|Children with quiet rooms, books, internet and educated parents have an advantage over those without;What is the writer's final opinion?|Homework should not be banned but should be short, purposeful and fair");

S(18, "The Lighthouse at Manora", `The lighthouse on Manora Island had guided ships into Karachi harbour for more than a century. For the last thirty of those years, its keeper had been Saleem's grandfather.

Saleem had spent every school holiday there, climbing the spiral staircase, polishing the great lens, and listening to his grandfather's stories of storms and shipwrecks. He loved the place with an intensity that sometimes surprised him. From the gallery at the top, the whole harbour spread out below like a map: cargo ships, fishing boats, the distant towers of the city shimmering in the heat.

So when the letter arrived announcing that the lighthouse would be automated, he felt as though something had been stolen.

"Automated?" he said. "What does that mean?"

"It means," said his grandfather, folding the letter carefully, "that a machine will do my job. The lamp will turn on and off by itself. Sensors will check everything and send the information to an office in the city. They won't need a keeper anymore."

"But that's not fair," Saleem protested. "You've done this all your life."

His grandfather looked out of the window at the sea. "Fair," he said thoughtfully, "is a complicated word."

Saleem waited, but the old man said nothing more. That evening, Saleem lay awake, composing angry letters in his head to the harbour authorities.

The following morning, his grandfather took him to the top of the tower. He pointed at the horizon, where a container ship was slowly approaching.

"When I started," he said, "I had to wind the clockwork mechanism every four hours, all night, to keep the light turning. If I fell asleep, the light stopped, and a ship might run aground. Twice in my first year, I almost did fall asleep. I was terrified."

He paused. "The new system will never fall asleep. It will never be ill. It will warn the harbour office instantly if something goes wrong. For the sailors out there, it is safer."

Saleem frowned. "But what about you?"

His grandfather smiled. "I'm sixty-eight. I've been waiting for a reason to stop." He looked at Saleem. "Change often feels like loss when it happens. Sometimes it is loss. But we must ask who benefits, and who pays the price, before we decide whether it's right."

Saleem thought about the sailors on the container ship, whose lives depended on the light. He thought about his grandfather, who had spent thirty years of nights awake.

"The lighthouse won't be the same," he said quietly.

"No," his grandfather agreed. "But it was never really about the building. It was about the light reaching the ships. That part won't change."

On the last night before the machines took over, Saleem and his grandfather sat together at the top of the tower and watched the beam sweep across the dark water, as it had done every night for a hundred years. Neither of them spoke. Neither of them needed to.`, "spiral, intensity, automated, sensors, protested, composing, mechanism, aground",
"Why was the lighthouse being automated?|A machine could run it safely without a keeper and never fall asleep or be ill;Why was Saleem upset at first?|He thought it was unfair that his grandfather's lifelong job was being taken away;What did the grandfather mean that it was never about the building?|What mattered was the light keeping sailors safe, and that would continue");

S(18, "The Number Zero", `Imagine trying to write the number two thousand and five without the digit zero. It would be extraordinarily difficult. Yet for most of human history, people managed without it. The invention of zero, both as a placeholder and as a number in its own right, is one of the most important ideas in the history of mathematics.

Early civilisations used various systems for counting. The ancient Romans used letters: I for one, V for five, X for ten, C for a hundred, M for a thousand. Two thousand and five was written MMV. This worked reasonably well for recording numbers, but calculation was painfully slow. Try multiplying MMV by XLVII, and you will understand why Roman merchants relied on counting boards and pebbles.

The Babylonians, who lived in what is now Iraq around four thousand years ago, developed a place-value system, in which the position of a symbol determined its value, as it does in our system today. However, they had no consistent way to show an empty place. Sometimes they left a gap, which easily led to confusion.

The breakthrough came in India. By around the fifth century, Indian mathematicians were using a system of nine digits plus a dot, and later a small circle, to show an empty position. In 628, the astronomer and mathematician Brahmagupta went further. He wrote rules for calculating with zero as a number: that a number minus itself equals zero, and that any number multiplied by zero equals zero. He even attempted to explain division by zero, a problem mathematicians would continue to debate for centuries.

The Indian word for zero was shunya, meaning emptiness. When these ideas reached Baghdad in the eighth century, Arab scholars translated shunya as sifr. In the ninth century, the Persian mathematician Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, who worked at the famous House of Wisdom in Baghdad, wrote a book explaining the Indian number system. His book was later translated into Latin, and through it Europeans learned about the new numerals. The word algorithm comes from a Latin version of al-Khwarizmi's name, and the word algebra comes from the title of another of his books.

Europeans were slow to accept the new system. Some merchants feared that the digits were too easy to forge, since a zero could be changed into a six or a nine. In 1299, the city of Florence even banned their use in official accounts. But the advantages were overwhelming. By the sixteenth century, Hindu-Arabic numerals had replaced Roman numerals throughout most of Europe for calculation.

The Latin word for sifr became zephirum, which eventually became zero in English. The same Arabic root also produced the word cipher.

Today, zero is everywhere. Every computer in the world operates using only two digits, zero and one. Without zero, there would be no modern science, no engineering, no internet. It is remarkable to think that one of humanity's most powerful ideas is, quite literally, the idea of nothing.`, "placeholder, civilisations, calculation, consistent, Brahmagupta, numerals, overwhelming, literally",
"Where did zero as a number first develop?|In India, with rules written by Brahmagupta in 628;How did the Indian number system reach Europe?|Through Baghdad, where al-Khwarizmi wrote about it, and his book was translated into Latin;Why does the writer say zero is the idea of nothing?|Zero means emptiness, yet this idea of nothing became hugely powerful");

S(18, "The Glaciers Are Speaking", `High in the mountains of northern Pakistan, where three of the world's great mountain ranges meet, lies one of the largest collections of ice outside the polar regions. The Karakoram, Himalaya and Hindu Kush together contain more than seven thousand glaciers. These rivers of ice, some of them more than sixty kilometres long, are sometimes called the water towers of Asia.

The glaciers matter far beyond the mountains. Each summer, as the upper layers melt, their water feeds the Indus River and its tributaries. That water flows hundreds of kilometres south to irrigate the farms of Punjab and Sindh, which grow much of the country's wheat, rice, cotton and sugarcane. It supplies drinking water to cities and turns the turbines of hydroelectric dams. In a very real sense, much of Pakistan's life depends on ice that most Pakistanis will never see.

Now the glaciers are changing. As the Earth's average temperature rises, mainly because of greenhouse gases released by burning coal, oil and gas, glaciers in many mountain regions are shrinking. Scientists have measured many Himalayan and Hindu Kush glaciers retreating, losing thickness and length year by year.

Interestingly, for many years some Karakoram glaciers seemed to be stable or even growing, a puzzle scientists called the Karakoram anomaly. The reasons are still debated, but they may involve local weather patterns and increased winter snowfall. Recent studies suggest that even here, the situation may be beginning to change.

Melting glaciers bring both immediate and long-term dangers. In the short term, meltwater can collect behind natural dams of ice and rock, forming glacial lakes. If such a dam suddenly bursts, a devastating flood called a glacial lake outburst flood can rush down the valley, destroying homes, bridges, roads and fields in minutes. In recent years, villages in Gilgit-Baltistan and Chitral have experienced several of these floods. In 2022, a bridge on the Karakoram Highway at Hassanabad was swept away by one.

In the long term, the danger is the opposite: too little water. At first, faster melting means more water in the rivers. But as glaciers shrink, there will eventually be less ice left to melt. Scientists warn that by the end of this century, summer river flows could decline, affecting the millions of people who depend on them.

What can be done? Some solutions are global. The most important step is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions worldwide, which requires cooperation between all countries. Pakistan produces only a small fraction of global emissions, yet it is among the countries most vulnerable to climate change, which many people see as deeply unfair.

Other solutions are local. Early warning systems with sensors and sirens can alert villages when a glacial lake is about to burst. Better water storage and more efficient irrigation can help farmers use every drop wisely. Planting trees on mountain slopes can reduce landslides.

The glaciers have existed for thousands of years. They are now sending us a message. Whether we listen may determine the future of millions of people downstream.`, "glaciers, tributaries, irrigate, hydroelectric, anomaly, devastating, emissions, vulnerable",
"Why are the northern glaciers called water towers?|Their meltwater feeds the Indus and its rivers, which supply farms, cities and dams;What is a glacial lake outburst flood?|When a natural dam holding back a glacial lake bursts and floods the valley;Why do many people see the situation as unfair?|Pakistan produces only a small share of emissions but is among the most affected");

S(18, "The Chess Match", `Rehan had never lost a chess game to anyone his own age. At fourteen, he was the junior champion of his district, and he carried that title with a confidence that some people called arrogance. When he walked into the provincial tournament in Lahore, he was expected to win easily.

His first four games went exactly as planned. He crushed each opponent in under an hour, barely looking up from the board. Then, in the semi-final, he was paired with a girl he had never heard of: Zara, from a small town near Sargodha. She was twelve, quiet, and wore a slightly oversized school sweater.

Rehan smiled to himself. This would be quick.

It was not quick.

Zara played cautiously at first, building a solid structure of pawns. Rehan attacked aggressively, as he always did, sacrificing a knight to open up her king. It was a bold move that had won him dozens of games.

Zara studied the board for a long time. Then she declined his sacrifice, quietly moving her king to safety. Within ten moves, Rehan realised with a cold feeling in his stomach that his attack had failed. He was a piece down, with no compensation.

He fought desperately for two more hours. But Zara made no mistakes. Finally, Rehan tipped over his king, the traditional sign of resignation, and shook her hand without meeting her eyes.

He spent the rest of the afternoon sulking in the corridor. When his coach found him, Rehan said bitterly, "She was lucky."

His coach sat beside him. "Was she?" he asked. "Or did she prepare better?"

Rehan said nothing.

"I spoke to her coach," his coach continued. "She has no computer at home. She learned chess from her grandfather, using a board with two missing pieces replaced by bottle caps. She studies games from a single old book, over and over. She has played through every one of your recorded games from the district championship."

Rehan looked up, surprised.

"She knew you would sacrifice the knight," his coach said gently. "Because you always do. You're a talented player, Rehan. But talent makes people lazy. You stopped thinking about your opponents because you assumed they were weaker than you."

Rehan sat with that for a long time. It hurt more than the defeat itself, because he knew it was true.

Later, he watched Zara play the final against a much older boy. She lost, narrowly, after a five-hour battle. When it was over, Rehan walked across the hall and sat down opposite her.

"Will you show me," he asked, "how you knew my plan?"

Zara looked at him warily, as if expecting a trick. Then she began to set up the pieces.

They talked for an hour. Before they left, they exchanged addresses and promised to send each other games by post and later by message. Over the next year, they played hundreds of games together. Both of them improved enormously. The following year, they met again in the provincial final. Rehan won. But he never again assumed that any opponent was weaker than he was.`, "provincial, arrogance, cautiously, aggressively, sacrificing, compensation, resignation, warily",
"How did Zara know Rehan's plan?|She studied all his recorded games and knew he always sacrificed the knight;What did the coach mean that talent makes people lazy?|Rehan relied on his talent and stopped thinking carefully about his opponents;How did Rehan change after losing?|He learned from Zara, became her friend and practice partner, and never underestimated anyone again");

S(18, "Ibn al-Haytham and the Science of Light", `For more than a thousand years, scholars argued about how human beings see. One popular theory, supported by the ancient Greek thinkers Euclid and Ptolemy, claimed that the eye sent out invisible rays, rather like a torch, which touched objects and allowed us to perceive them. Another theory suggested that objects sent out tiny copies of themselves that flew into the eye.

The person who finally settled the question was a scientist named Abu Ali al-Hasan ibn al-Haytham, born around 965 in Basra, in what is now Iraq. In Europe, he later became known as Alhazen.

According to a famous story, Ibn al-Haytham was invited to Cairo by the powerful ruler al-Hakim, who wanted him to find a way to control the flooding of the Nile. After travelling up the river, Ibn al-Haytham realised that the project was impossible with the technology of the time. Fearing the ruler's anger, he is said to have pretended to be mad, and he was confined to his house for about ten years. Whether or not every detail is true, historians agree that he produced much of his greatest work during this period.

His most important book, the Book of Optics, completed around 1021, transformed the study of light and vision. Ibn al-Haytham argued that light travels in straight lines from objects into the eye, and not the other way around. He explained that every point on an object reflects light in all directions, and the eye gathers some of these rays to form an image.

What made his work revolutionary was not only his conclusions but the way he reached them. Rather than relying on the authority of famous ancient writers, he insisted on testing ideas through careful, repeatable experiments. He wrote that a seeker of truth should not simply trust what earlier authors had said, but should question and test everything, including his own ideas.

One of his most famous experiments involved a darkened room with a tiny hole in one wall. Light passing through the hole projected an upside-down image of the scene outside onto the opposite wall. This device, called a camera obscura, from the Latin for dark room, demonstrated that light travels in straight lines. It is also the basic principle behind every camera ever made, including the one in your phone.

Ibn al-Haytham also studied reflection in mirrors, refraction, which is the bending of light as it passes from air into water or glass, the structure of the eye, and why the moon appears larger near the horizon. His method of observation, hypothesis and experiment is very similar to what we now call the scientific method.

His Book of Optics was translated into Latin around 1200 and influenced European scientists for centuries, including Roger Bacon, Johannes Kepler and others who helped build modern science.

Today, many historians describe Ibn al-Haytham as one of the first true experimental scientists. His life reminds us that great discoveries require not only intelligence, but also the courage to question what everyone else believes.`, "perceive, confined, optics, revolutionary, authority, obscura, refraction, hypothesis",
"What did Ibn al-Haytham prove about vision?|Light travels in straight lines from objects into the eye, not out of the eye;Why was his method revolutionary?|He tested ideas with careful experiments instead of trusting famous ancient writers;What is refraction?|The bending of light as it passes from one material, like air, into another, like water or glass");

S(18, "The Printing Press", `Before the fifteenth century, almost every book in Europe was copied by hand. A skilled scribe, often a monk, might spend months or even years copying a single book with a pen and ink. Books were so rare and expensive that a large library might contain only a few hundred volumes, and most ordinary people never owned one in their lives.

Around 1440, in the German city of Mainz, a goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg began working on an invention that would change the world. His idea was to combine several existing technologies into a single system. He made small individual metal letters, called movable type, which could be arranged to form words and lines, inked, and pressed onto paper using a modified wine press. After printing, the letters could be taken apart and rearranged to print a completely different page.

Gutenberg was not the first person to invent movable type. In China, an inventor named Bi Sheng had created movable type from baked clay around the 1040s, and in Korea, a Buddhist text called the Jikji was printed with metal type in 1377. However, Chinese and Korean writing uses thousands of different characters, which made movable type less practical. The European alphabet had only a few dozen letters, so Gutenberg's system was far more efficient for European languages.

Gutenberg's most famous work was a Bible completed around 1455. About 180 copies were printed, and around forty-nine survive today. They are among the most valuable books in the world.

The impact of the printing press was astonishing. Within fifty years, printing shops had spread to more than two hundred European cities, and millions of books had been printed. The cost of books fell dramatically. For the first time, merchants, craftspeople and even some farmers could afford to own them.

This transformed society in ways no one could have predicted. Scientific discoveries could now be shared quickly and accurately, allowing scholars in different countries to build on each other's work. Before printing, every hand-copied book introduced new errors. Printed books were identical, so a diagram or table in one copy matched every other copy exactly.

Printing also helped spread new religious and political ideas, sometimes causing fierce conflict. Rulers and religious authorities who had controlled what people read suddenly found that pamphlets criticising them could be printed and distributed in thousands within weeks. Some tried to ban certain books, with limited success.

Literacy, the ability to read and write, gradually increased. Languages became more standardised, as printers chose particular spellings that readers across a region then learned.

Interestingly, printing spread much more slowly in the Ottoman Empire and much of the Muslim world, where beautiful handwriting, or calligraphy, was deeply respected, and where many scribes earned their living by copying books. Printing in Arabic script only became widespread in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.

Many historians compare the printing press to the internet. Both made information cheaper, faster and more widely available. Both brought great benefits and new dangers, including the rapid spread of false information. Thinking about how people adapted to the printing press may help us understand how to live wisely with the technologies of our own time.`, "scribe, volumes, movable, efficient, dramatically, pamphlets, literacy, standardised",
"Why did Gutenberg's movable type work well in Europe?|The European alphabet had only a few dozen letters, unlike Chinese with thousands of characters;How did printing help science?|Discoveries could be shared quickly, and printed copies were identical without copying errors;Why does the writer compare the printing press to the internet?|Both made information cheaper and faster to share and brought both benefits and dangers");

S(18, "The Last Potter of Hala", `In the town of Hala in Sindh, famous for centuries for its glazed blue pottery, there had once been more than forty potters' workshops. Now there was only one that still used the old methods. It belonged to Ustad Ghulam Rasool, who was seventy-four, and whose hands, though wrinkled and spotted, still moved over the wheel with astonishing precision.

His grandson Bilal, who was fifteen, helped in the workshop after school. He mixed the clay, carried water, and fed the kiln with wood. He knew how much his grandfather wanted him to continue the family tradition. He also knew that he didn't want to.

Bilal wanted to study computer science. He had taught himself to code on a borrowed phone, and his teacher said he had a remarkable gift for it. He dreamed of a university in Karachi and a job designing software.

He hadn't told his grandfather. Every time he tried, the words stuck in his throat.

One evening, a group of foreign tourists arrived with a guide. They admired the pottery, took photographs, and bought several pieces. One of them, a woman who spoke a little Urdu, asked Ustad Ghulam Rasool who would continue his work after him.

The old man glanced at Bilal and smiled. "My grandson," he said proudly.

Bilal felt sick. That night, after the workshop closed, he finally spoke.

"Dada," he said, his voice trembling. "I don't want to be a potter. I want to study computers."

The silence that followed seemed endless. His grandfather put down the cup he was holding and stared at the cold kiln for a long time.

"My father was a potter," he said finally. "And his father, and his father before him. For seven generations, our hands have shaped this clay."

"I know," Bilal whispered. "I'm sorry."

His grandfather turned to him. His face was difficult to read. "Do you know why there are no other potters left in Hala?" he asked. "Because their sons and grandsons all left. They went to the cities, to the Gulf, to work in offices and factories. I was angry with them for a long time. I thought they were betraying something sacred."

Bilal waited.

"But I was wrong to be angry," the old man said slowly. "A tradition that survives only because people are forced to continue it is not really alive. It is just a prison."

Bilal looked at him in astonishment.

"Go and study," his grandfather said. "But I ask one thing. Before you go, let me teach you everything I know. Not so you will become a potter. So that the knowledge is not lost."

For the next two years, Bilal spent every afternoon learning. He filmed his grandfather at work, recording every technique, every glaze recipe, every secret of the kiln. Then he did something his grandfather had never imagined: he built a website and an online archive, with videos explaining the craft of Hala pottery in Sindhi, Urdu and English.

Within a year, the videos had been watched by thousands of people. Three young people from Hala came to the workshop asking to learn. Ustad Ghulam Rasool took them on as apprentices.

When Bilal left for university in Karachi, his grandfather gave him a small blue bowl he had made especially for him. It still sits on Bilal's desk, beside his computer.`, "glazed, precision, generations, betraying, sacred, tradition, archive, apprentices",
"Why was Bilal afraid to tell his grandfather the truth?|He knew his grandfather wanted him to continue the seven-generation family tradition;What did the grandfather mean that a forced tradition is a prison?|A tradition only truly lives if people choose to continue it, not if they are forced;How did Bilal help save the craft without becoming a potter?|He recorded his grandfather's knowledge in videos and an online archive, which inspired new apprentices");

S(18, "Are Smartphones Good for Teenagers?", `In just a few years, smartphones have become an ordinary part of life for millions of teenagers around the world, including in Pakistan. They are used for messaging friends, watching videos, playing games, doing homework and much more. But as their use has grown, so has a fierce debate: are smartphones good or bad for young people?

Supporters of smartphones argue that they offer enormous benefits. First, they provide access to information and education. A student in a remote village with a phone and an internet connection can watch science lessons, look up unfamiliar words, practise English, or learn to code. Free online courses have given opportunities to young people who might never have had them otherwise.

Second, phones help young people stay connected with family and friends, particularly when relatives live far away or work abroad. For teenagers who feel isolated or different, online communities can provide a sense of belonging and support.

Third, phones can be useful for safety. Parents can contact their children, and teenagers can call for help in an emergency.

On the other hand, critics have raised serious concerns. Many researchers have found links between heavy smartphone use and problems such as poor sleep, difficulty concentrating, and anxiety. When teenagers use their phones late at night, the bright screens and constant notifications can disturb their sleep, which is essential for learning and health.

Social media brings particular risks. Many apps are deliberately designed to keep users scrolling for as long as possible, using techniques such as endless feeds and unpredictable rewards. Some teenagers compare themselves constantly with carefully edited images of others, which can damage their self-esteem. Cyberbullying, in which people are harassed or humiliated online, can follow a young person everywhere, even into their bedroom at night.

Critics also worry about what smartphones replace. Hours spent on screens may mean fewer hours spent reading, playing outdoors, talking face to face, or simply being bored, which some psychologists believe is important for creativity.

It is important to note that scientists still disagree about how strong some of these effects are, and whether phones cause problems or whether teenagers who already have problems tend to use phones more. Research is ongoing.

So what is the sensible conclusion? It seems clear that smartphones are tools, and like most tools, their effects depend on how they are used. A phone used to learn a new skill or call a grandparent is very different from a phone used to scroll for five hours at midnight.

Some schools have banned phones during the school day, and many report better concentration and more conversation at break times. Some families agree on rules, such as no phones at meals or charging phones outside bedrooms at night.

Perhaps the most important skill for young people today is learning to control their technology, rather than allowing it to control them. Before reaching for your phone, it can be useful to ask a simple question: am I choosing to use this, or am I just doing it out of habit?`, "remote, belonging, concentrating, notifications, deliberately, self-esteem, cyberbullying, psychologists",
"What are two benefits of smartphones mentioned?|Access to education and information, staying connected with family and friends, and safety;Why can social media be harmful?|Apps are designed to keep people scrolling, comparisons can hurt self-esteem, and cyberbullying can follow you;What does the writer mean that phones are tools?|Whether they help or harm depends on how they are used");

S(18, "Black Holes", `Imagine an object so dense that nothing, not even light, can escape its gravitational pull. Such objects really exist, and they are called black holes. For decades, they were among the most mysterious ideas in physics. Today, astronomers have not only detected them but have even taken pictures of them.

To understand a black hole, we must first understand gravity. Every object with mass pulls on other objects. The more mass an object has, and the more tightly that mass is packed together, the stronger its gravity is near its surface. To escape the Earth's gravity, a rocket must travel at about eleven kilometres per second. This is called escape velocity.

Now imagine squeezing the entire mass of the Earth into a ball the size of a marble. Its gravity near the surface would become so strong that the escape velocity would be greater than the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second. Since nothing can travel faster than light, nothing could escape. That is a black hole.

The boundary around a black hole, beyond which nothing can return, is called the event horizon. Anything that crosses it, whether a star, a planet or a beam of light, is trapped forever.

Most black holes form when very massive stars, many times heavier than our Sun, reach the end of their lives. When such a star runs out of fuel, it can no longer support itself against its own gravity. Its core collapses inward in a fraction of a second, while its outer layers are blasted into space in a gigantic explosion called a supernova. If the remaining core is heavy enough, it collapses into a black hole.

There are also supermassive black holes, millions or even billions of times heavier than the Sun, found at the centres of most large galaxies. Our own galaxy, the Milky Way, has one at its centre, called Sagittarius A star, with a mass about four million times that of the Sun. How these giants formed is still one of the great questions in astronomy.

If black holes are invisible, how do scientists know they are there? They observe their effects. Stars orbiting an invisible object at incredible speeds reveal its mass. Gas falling toward a black hole is heated to millions of degrees and glows brightly before it disappears. In 2015, scientists detected gravitational waves, tiny ripples in space itself, produced when two black holes collided over a billion years ago.

In April 2019, an international team of scientists using a network of radio telescopes across the world, called the Event Horizon Telescope, released the first ever image of a black hole. It showed a glowing ring of gas surrounding a dark centre in a galaxy called M87, about fifty-five million light years away. In 2022, they released an image of the black hole at the centre of our own galaxy.

Black holes remain full of mysteries. Scientists still debate what happens at their very centres, where our current theories of physics break down. Studying them may one day help answer some of the deepest questions about space, time, and the nature of the universe itself.`, "gravitational, velocity, horizon, massive, supernova, supermassive, telescopes, collided",
"What makes a black hole different from other objects?|Its gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape;How do scientists detect black holes if they are invisible?|By watching fast-orbiting stars, glowing gas falling in, and gravitational waves;What is the event horizon?|The boundary around a black hole beyond which nothing can return");

S(18, "The Witness", `Usman saw it happen from the window of the bus.

It was a Tuesday morning, and the bus had stopped at a busy junction in Rawalpindi. A boy on a bicycle, perhaps eleven years old, was crossing the road when a white car turned the corner too fast. The car clipped the back wheel of the bicycle, and the boy fell heavily onto the road.

The car slowed for a moment. Usman saw the driver's face clearly through the windscreen: a young man with a thin beard and sunglasses, talking on his phone. Then the car accelerated and disappeared into the traffic.

People rushed to help the boy. He was crying and holding his arm, but he was conscious. Someone called an ambulance. The bus pulled away before Usman could see anything more.

He couldn't stop thinking about it all day. In the afternoon, he heard from a classmate that the boy had broken his arm and injured his head, and that the police were asking for witnesses. Nobody had noted the car's number plate, but Usman had. He had seen it clearly and had repeated it to himself without even meaning to.

That evening, he told his father.

His father's expression grew serious. "Are you sure about the number?" he asked.

"Yes."

His father was quiet for a long time. Then he said, "Usman, going to the police can be complicated. You'd have to give a statement. You might have to go to court. And we don't know who this man is. If he's from a powerful family, it could cause trouble for us."

Usman hadn't thought about that. He felt a flicker of fear.

His mother, who had been listening from the kitchen, came and sat down. "And if it were Usman lying in the road," she said quietly, "wouldn't we want someone to come forward?"

His father looked at her, then at his son. The argument went on late into the night. Usman went to bed confused. He understood his father's worries. They were not cowardly, just cautious. But he kept seeing the boy lying on the road and the driver's face as he drove away.

In the morning, Usman made his decision. He went to his father before school.

"I'm going to tell the police," he said. "I'll understand if you're angry. But I don't think I could live with myself if I stayed silent."

His father looked at him for a long time. Then, unexpectedly, he stood up and put on his jacket. "Then I'm coming with you," he said. "You won't do this alone."

At the police station, Usman gave his statement while his father sat beside him. The officer wrote down the number plate carefully.

Three weeks later, they learned that the driver had been identified and charged. He had previously been fined several times for dangerous driving. The injured boy's family came to Usman's house with a box of sweets. The boy, his arm still in a cast, shook Usman's hand shyly.

That night, Usman's father said, "I was afraid. But you were right. Courage isn't the absence of fear. It's doing the right thing even when you're frightened."`, "junction, accelerated, conscious, witnesses, statement, cautious, identified, absence",
"Why was Usman's father worried about going to the police?|It could be complicated, involve court, and the driver might be from a powerful family;What made Usman decide to speak up?|He kept thinking of the injured boy and couldn't live with staying silent;What did his father mean that courage isn't the absence of fear?|Being brave means doing the right thing even when you are afraid");

S(18, "Arfa Karim", `In 2004, a nine-year-old girl from a village near Faisalabad became the youngest person in the world to earn a Microsoft Certified Professional qualification, a difficult technical exam usually taken by adults working in the computer industry. Her name was Arfa Karim Randhawa, and her remarkable achievement made her a national hero in Pakistan.

Arfa was born in 1995 in Ram Dewali, a small village in Punjab. Her family had a background in the army and farming. From an early age, she was unusually curious and quick to learn. When she was given access to a computer, she became fascinated, spending hours learning how software worked.

Her father encouraged her, and after several months of study, she passed the exam. The news spread quickly around the world. Bill Gates, the founder of Microsoft, invited her to visit the company's headquarters in the United States, where she met him personally. She spent some time there, and when she returned home, she was celebrated in newspapers and on television.

Arfa received a number of awards, including the Fatima Jinnah Gold Medal for science and technology and the President's Award for Pride of Performance, one of the country's highest honours. She was invited to speak at technology conferences, where she impressed audiences with her confidence and clear thinking. She talked about her dream of using technology to improve education and opportunities for children in Pakistan, particularly girls in rural areas.

What made Arfa inspiring was not only her intelligence. She was also known for her humility and her belief that her achievements belonged to her country and not just to herself. She said she hoped that thousands of other Pakistani children would follow in her footsteps.

Tragically, Arfa's life was very short. In December 2011, at just sixteen years old, she suffered a severe seizure and cardiac arrest, which caused serious brain damage. She was admitted to hospital in Lahore, where she remained in intensive care for several weeks. Bill Gates personally offered to help with her treatment. Despite the efforts of her doctors, she died in January 2012.

Her death caused great sadness across Pakistan. Thousands of people attended her funeral, and messages of grief came from around the world.

To honour her memory, the government of Punjab renamed a major technology park in Lahore the Arfa Software Technology Park. It is now home to many technology companies. Several schools, computer laboratories and scholarships have also been named after her.

Arfa Karim's story raises important questions. How many other talented children, especially in villages, never get the chance to discover their abilities because they lack access to computers, good teachers, or encouragement? Arfa had a father who believed in her and a family that gave her opportunities. Many children do not.

Her legacy is perhaps best understood not as the story of one extraordinary girl, but as a reminder of what becomes possible when young people, wherever they come from, are given the chance to learn.`, "qualification, technical, fascinated, headquarters, humility, seizure, intensive, legacy",
"What did Arfa achieve at age nine?|She became the youngest Microsoft Certified Professional in the world;How was Arfa remembered after her death?|A technology park in Lahore, schools, labs and scholarships were named after her;What question does the writer say her story raises?|How many talented children never get the chance to discover their abilities because they lack opportunities");

S(18, "The Volunteer", `When the floods came in the summer of 2022, Ammar was sixteen and had spent his whole life in Karachi. He had seen the pictures on television: villages swallowed by brown water, families sitting on the roofs of houses, children wading through water up to their chests. A third of the country was under water. More than thirty million people had lost their homes or their livelihoods.

He felt helpless. His school organised a collection of clothes and food, and he gave what he could. But it didn't feel like enough.

Then his uncle, a doctor, announced that he was joining a medical team travelling to a flood-affected district in Sindh. Ammar begged to go with him.

His mother refused. "It's dangerous," she said. "There's disease. And what can a sixteen-year-old do?"

But Ammar persisted for three days, and eventually, with his uncle's promise to keep him close, she reluctantly agreed.

Nothing on television had prepared him for what he saw. The road ended abruptly where the water began. Beyond it, as far as he could see, was a flat brown lake, with the tops of trees and electricity poles poking through the surface. The camp was a crowded field of tents on a stretch of raised ground. The smell of mud, smoke and sickness hung in the air.

Ammar had imagined himself doing something heroic. Instead, his uncle gave him the most ordinary jobs. He sorted boxes of medicines, filled water containers, and wrote down the names and ages of patients as they waited in a long, slow line under the burning sun.

At first, he felt frustrated. Then, gradually, he began to understand that these small tasks mattered. Because the medicines were sorted, the doctors could find them quickly. Because the patients' details were recorded, children who needed follow-up care could be found again.

He also discovered something unexpected. Many of the people waiting simply wanted someone to talk to. An old man told him about the buffalo he had lost and the house his father had built. A young mother showed him a photograph of her village before the floods. A group of children followed him everywhere, asking questions about Karachi and laughing at his attempts to speak Sindhi.

One afternoon, a girl of about nine asked him if he could help her read a letter from a relief organisation. Her parents couldn't read. Ammar read it aloud, explaining the instructions for collecting food rations. After that, people came to him every day with letters and forms.

After three weeks, it was time to go home. On his last day, the children gathered around him. The girl whose letter he had read gave him a drawing: a house standing on dry land, with the sun shining above it.

Back in Karachi, Ammar found it difficult to explain the experience to his friends. He hadn't saved anyone's life. He hadn't done anything that would appear on television.

But he had learned that helping is rarely dramatic. Most of the time, it consists of small, patient acts, done carefully, again and again, by ordinary people who decide to show up.`, "livelihoods, persisted, reluctantly, abruptly, heroic, frustrated, relief, rations",
"Why did Ammar feel frustrated at first?|He had imagined doing something heroic, but he was given ordinary jobs;How did Ammar's small jobs actually help?|Sorted medicines let doctors work fast, and recorded details meant children could get follow-up care;What did Ammar learn about helping?|It is rarely dramatic and usually means small, patient acts done again and again");

S(18, "The Leaf That Feeds the World", `Almost every living thing on Earth depends, directly or indirectly, on a single process taking place quietly inside green leaves. It is called photosynthesis, from Greek words meaning putting together with light. Without it, there would be no food, no oxygen to breathe, and almost no life at all.

Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae and some bacteria use the energy of sunlight to make their own food. They take in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny pores on the undersides of their leaves called stomata. They absorb water from the soil through their roots, which travels up through the stem to the leaves. Then, using the energy of sunlight, they combine carbon dioxide and water to produce glucose, a type of sugar, and release oxygen as a by-product.

The key to this process is a green pigment called chlorophyll, found inside tiny structures in plant cells called chloroplasts. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light very well, but reflects green light, which is why leaves look green to our eyes.

The glucose that plants produce has several uses. Some is used immediately for energy. Some is converted into starch, which is stored in roots, seeds and fruits. Some is used to make cellulose, the tough material that forms plant cell walls and gives wood its strength.

This is why photosynthesis matters so much to us. When we eat rice, wheat, potatoes or mangoes, we are eating energy that the plant captured from sunlight. Even when we eat meat, eggs or milk, that energy originally came from plants that the animals ate. Every food chain on land begins with photosynthesis.

The oxygen released by photosynthesis is equally important. Billions of years ago, Earth's atmosphere contained almost no free oxygen. Tiny ocean organisms called cyanobacteria began photosynthesising and gradually filled the atmosphere with oxygen over hundreds of millions of years. This made it possible for oxygen-breathing animals, including humans, to evolve.

Today, scientists estimate that around half of the oxygen produced on Earth comes from microscopic algae and plankton in the oceans, while the rest comes mostly from land plants, including vast forests.

Photosynthesis also plays a crucial role in the climate. Plants absorb carbon dioxide, one of the main greenhouse gases responsible for global warming. Forests, grasslands and oceans store huge amounts of carbon. When forests are cut down or burned, much of that carbon returns to the atmosphere, making climate change worse.

This is one reason why tree-planting campaigns have become popular around the world, including in Pakistan, where a large programme aimed to plant billions of trees. However, scientists point out that planting trees alone cannot solve climate change. The trees must be the right species for the area, they must survive and grow for many decades, and reducing emissions from fossil fuels remains essential.

Scientists are also studying photosynthesis in order to improve it. If crops could photosynthesise more efficiently, they might produce more food using less land and water, which would be enormously valuable as the world's population grows.

The next time you sit under a tree, remember that its leaves are quietly performing one of the most important chemical processes on the planet.`, "photosynthesis, stomata, glucose, chlorophyll, cellulose, cyanobacteria, plankton, efficiently",
"What do plants need for photosynthesis?|Sunlight, carbon dioxide from the air, and water from the soil;Why does every food chain on land begin with photosynthesis?|Plants capture energy from sunlight, and animals and people get that energy by eating plants or animals that ate plants;Why do leaves look green?|Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light but reflects green light");

S(18, "The Karakoram Highway", `Stretching for about thirteen hundred kilometres from Hasan Abdal, near Islamabad, to Kashgar in western China, the Karakoram Highway is one of the highest paved international roads in the world. At its highest point, the Khunjerab Pass, it climbs to about 4,700 metres above sea level, where the air contains only about half as much oxygen as at sea level. Many people call it the eighth wonder of the world.

Before the highway was built, the remote valleys of Gilgit, Hunza and the far north were connected to the rest of Pakistan only by narrow, dangerous mule tracks. A journey that now takes a day by road could take weeks. Food, medicine and supplies were scarce and expensive. In winter, many areas were completely cut off.

Construction began in 1959 and continued for around twenty years, through cooperation between Pakistan and China. Thousands of Pakistani and Chinese workers took part, many of them soldiers and engineers. They worked with dynamite, bulldozers, and often simple tools, carving a road through some of the most unstable terrain on Earth.

The challenges were immense. The road follows the gorges of the Indus and Hunza rivers, where cliffs rise vertically for hundreds of metres. The mountains of the region are geologically young and still rising, because the Indian tectonic plate continues to push northward into Asia. As a result, rockfalls, landslides and earthquakes are frequent. In winter, temperatures fall far below freezing and avalanches block the road. In summer, melting glaciers swell the rivers and cause floods.

The human cost was high. Hundreds of workers lost their lives during construction, killed by falling rocks, accidents and landslides. It is often said that one person died for every kilometre of road built. Memorials and graveyards along the highway honour them.

The highway was completed in the late 1970s and opened to the public in the 1980s. Its effects on the northern areas were enormous. Trade increased, tourism developed, and goods became cheaper and more available. Students could travel to universities in other cities, and patients could reach hospitals. The road also strengthened economic ties between Pakistan and China.

However, the changes were not entirely positive. Some local people worried that their traditional cultures and languages would be weakened by the arrival of outsiders and modern goods. Increased traffic and construction also affected the fragile mountain environment.

The highway continues to face natural dangers. In 2010, a massive landslide near the village of Attabad blocked the Hunza River, creating a lake more than twenty kilometres long that submerged villages and a long section of the highway. For several years, vehicles and goods had to be carried across the lake by boat until new tunnels were built.

Today, the highway is being upgraded as part of larger projects linking Pakistan and China. Its story is one of extraordinary human determination, sacrifice and engineering skill, and also of the difficult balance between progress and protecting fragile places.`, "paved, scarce, cooperation, dynamite, terrain, tectonic, avalanches, submerged",
"Why was the Karakoram Highway so hard to build?|Steep cliffs, unstable young mountains, landslides, earthquakes, freezing winters and floods;How did the highway change life in the northern areas?|Trade, tourism, education and healthcare became easier, and goods became cheaper;What does the writer mean by the balance between progress and protecting fragile places?|Development brings benefits but can also harm local cultures and the environment");

S(18, "The Election", `When the school announced elections for head girl, everyone assumed Mahnoor would win. She was confident, popular, and the best public speaker in Class Nine. Her only serious rival was Sidra, who was clever and hardworking but so quiet that many students barely knew her.

Mahnoor's campaign was dazzling. She made colourful posters, organised a team of supporters, and promised exciting things: longer break times, a school trip to the mountains, a canteen selling pizza. Students cheered whenever she walked down the corridor.

Sidra's campaign was much simpler. She made a few plain posters and visited each class to listen to students' concerns. She learned that the girls' toilets on the second floor had been broken for months, that the library was closed most afternoons because there was no one to supervise it, and that some younger students were being bullied at the bus stop. She promised to work with teachers to fix these specific problems.

A week before the election, Mahnoor's best friend, Laiba, came to her with a piece of paper. "Look what I found," she said excitedly. It was a page torn from Sidra's diary, which had fallen out of her bag. On it, Sidra had written about her anxiety before speeches, how her hands shook and she sometimes felt sick. "If we share this," Laiba whispered, "everyone will see she's too nervous to be head girl."

Mahnoor looked at the page. It would be easy. One photograph, shared in the class group, and Sidra's chances would be finished.

She thought about it all evening. She told herself that politics was a competition, and that everyone used whatever advantages they had. She told herself that Sidra really might be too nervous for the job. But underneath these arguments, she felt something uncomfortable, a feeling she recognised as shame.

She imagined how she would feel if someone shared her private thoughts with the whole school. She imagined Sidra's face.

The next morning, she found Sidra alone in the library and handed her the page without a word.

Sidra stared at it, then at Mahnoor. Her face turned pale, then red. "Did anyone else see this?"

"Only Laiba. She won't share it. I've made sure."

Sidra's eyes filled with tears. "Why are you giving it back? You could have used it."

Mahnoor shrugged, uncomfortable. "If I win, I want it to be because people chose me. Not because I humiliated you."

On election day, Sidra stood before the whole school to give her speech. Her hands were visibly shaking. But she spoke clearly about the broken toilets, the closed library, and the bullying at the bus stop.

Mahnoor won the election, though by fewer votes than anyone expected. In her first speech as head girl, she announced that she had asked Sidra to be her deputy, responsible for fixing the problems she had identified. The audience applauded.

By the end of the year, the toilets were repaired, the library was open every afternoon with student volunteers, and teachers supervised the bus stop. When people praised Mahnoor's leadership, she always made sure they knew who had thought of those ideas first.`, "assumed, dazzling, supervise, anxiety, competition, humiliated, visibly, deputy",
"How was Sidra's campaign different from Mahnoor's?|Sidra listened to students and promised to fix specific real problems instead of making exciting promises;Why did Mahnoor give the diary page back?|She felt ashamed and wanted to win fairly, not by humiliating Sidra;What does Mahnoor choosing Sidra as deputy show?|She respected Sidra's good ideas and wanted to work together");

S(18, "Every Drop Counts", `Pakistan is often described as a country rich in water. The mighty Indus River and its tributaries flow through its heart, fed by glaciers and monsoon rains, supporting one of the largest irrigation systems in the world. Yet many experts warn that Pakistan is heading toward a serious water crisis. How can both things be true?

The answer lies partly in numbers. The amount of water available per person depends not only on how much water a country has, but on how many people share it. When Pakistan became independent in 1947, its population was around thirty million. Today, it is well over two hundred million, and still growing. The amount of water available for each person has fallen dramatically, and the country is now considered one of the most water-stressed in the world.

The way water is used matters too. More than ninety percent of Pakistan's water is used for agriculture. Much of it is lost before it reaches crops, leaking from unlined canals, evaporating in the heat, or soaking into the ground. Many farmers still flood their fields, a method that uses far more water than modern techniques such as drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to the roots of each plant. Some of the crops grown, such as rice and sugarcane, require very large amounts of water.

Groundwater is another concern. In many areas, farmers and cities pump water from beneath the ground using tube wells. Because this water is often free or very cheap, it is used faster than rain can refill it. In some places, the water table, the level at which groundwater is found, has fallen so far that wells must be dug deeper every year.

In the cities, the problems are different but equally serious. In Karachi, many neighbourhoods receive piped water only a few hours a week, if at all. Families must buy water from tanker trucks at high prices, while the poorest often pay the most. Meanwhile, old pipes leak, and water is sometimes stolen from the system.

Water quality is a further challenge. Many water sources are polluted by sewage, industrial waste and agricultural chemicals. Unsafe drinking water causes diseases such as diarrhoea and typhoid, which are especially dangerous for young children.

Climate change makes the situation more uncertain. Changing rainfall, more intense heatwaves, and shrinking glaciers could all affect how much water reaches the rivers in future.

Fortunately, there are many possible solutions. Lining canals could greatly reduce leakage. Encouraging efficient irrigation and crops that need less water could save enormous amounts. Repairing city pipes, collecting rainwater from rooftops, and treating and reusing wastewater could all help. Many experts also argue that water should be priced more fairly, so that people value it and do not waste it, while ensuring that the poorest can still afford enough.

Individuals can make a difference too: turning off taps while brushing teeth, fixing leaks, and reusing water for plants.

Water is often treated as if it were endless. The evidence suggests otherwise. Whether Pakistan faces a crisis or a secure future may depend on choices made in the next few decades.`, "tributaries, irrigation, independent, agriculture, evaporating, groundwater, polluted, wastewater",
"Why is Pakistan's water per person falling?|The population has grown from about thirty million to over two hundred million, sharing the same water;Why is a lot of farm water wasted?|Unlined canals leak, water evaporates, and fields are flooded instead of using methods like drip irrigation;What is the water table?|The level underground where groundwater is found");
